-- title:
--   Mumford fields of the level groups are curves over C
-- statement:
--   Fix rationals $a_1,b_1$, a non-zero squarefree natural number $N$ and primes $q,q'$ with $q\nmid N$, $q'\nmid N$, $q'\neq q$ and $q,q'\ge 5$, and write $\mathbb H=\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]$ for the associated quaternion algebra over $\mathbb Q$.
--
--   *Quaternionic data.* The hypothesis `hdef₁` asserts `IsDefiniteRamifiedExactlyAt a₁ b₁ q'`, i.e. $a_1<0$, $b_1<0$, and for every height-one prime $v$ of $\mathcal O_{\mathbb Q}$ the completion $\mathbb H\otimes_{\mathbb Q}\mathbb Q_v$ is a division algebra (every non-zero element is a unit) precisely when $q'\in v$; thus the distinguished finite ramification place is the one containing $q'$, not $q$. Two $\mathbb Z$-submodules $\Lambda_1,R_1$ of $\mathbb H$ are given with $\Lambda_1$ a maximal order (an order, maximal among the orders containing it), $R_1$ an Eichler order of level $N$ (an intersection of two maximal orders, of relative index $N$ in the first of them) and $R_1\le\Lambda_1$.
--
--   *Hecke data at $q$.* A unit $n_1$ of $\mathbb H\otimes_{\mathbb Q}\mathbb A^{\mathrm{fin}}_{\mathbb Q}$ lies in `primeHeckeSet R₁ q`: $n_1$ lies in the finite-adelic box of $R_1$ (the additive group generated by the $z\otimes a$ with $z\in R_1$ and $a$ integral at every place), $q\,n_1^{-1}$ lies in that box, while $n_1^{-1}$ and $q^{-1}n_1$ do not. Writing $S_1=$ `meetOrder R₁ n₁` $=R_1\cap n_1R_1n_1^{-1}$ (conjugation taken adelically), the hypotheses require: $S_1$ to be an Eichler order of level $Nq$ (`hS₁`); $S_1$ to be stable under adelic conjugation by $n_1$ (`hnorm₁`); and the shift $x\mapsto [\,x^{\mathrm{out}}n_1\,]$ on the class set of the finite-idelic stabiliser of $S_1$ to be an involution (`hsq₁`). The class sets of the stabilisers of $S_1$ and of $R_1$ are finite. The hypothesis `hlaws₁` is `ClassSetHeckeLaws N q Λ₁ R₁ n₁`: the edge Hecke matrices `classSetEdgeHecke N q Λ₁ R₁ n₁ ℓ` on the class set of the stabiliser of $S_1$ commute pairwise, the vertex Hecke matrices `classSetVertexHecke N Λ₁ R₁ ℓ` on the class set of the stabiliser of $R_1$ commute pairwise, for every prime $\ell\neq q$ both degeneracy pushforwards $\delta_i$ ($i\in\mathrm{Fin}\,2$, coming from the forgetful map and from the shift by $n_1$) carry the edge operator to the vertex operator, and the joint kernel of the $\delta_i$ is stable under every edge Hecke operator.
--
--   *The place above $q$ and the rigid-analytic data.* A valuation subring $A_2$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ is given with $q$ a non-unit of $A_2$ (`hA₂`), and the decomposition subgroup of $A_2$ over $\mathbb Q$ acts isometrically for the valuation of $A_2$ (`hiso₂`). Write $C=A_2$`.valuation.Completion` and $K_0=$ [`ValuationSubring.ratClosure A₂`](def/ValuationSubring_CompletionRatClosure.html#L13), the topological closure of the prime subfield inside $C$. A height-one prime $v_2$ of $\mathcal O_{\mathbb Q}$ contains $q$ (`hv₂`). An injective $\mathbb Q$-algebra map $\iota_2\colon\mathbb H\to M_2(K_0)$ is given, together with $\rho_2\colon\mathbb H^\times\to\mathrm{PGL}_2(K_0)$ which by `hρ₂` sends $x$ to the projective class of $\iota_2(x)$. Further, $\varpi_2$ is a pseudo-uniformizer for $(K_0,C)$ — an element of $K_0$ whose image in $C$ has valuation strictly between $0$ and $1$, with the scaling property that the valuation of every non-zero element of $K_0$ is squeezed between a power of $v(\varpi_2)$ and the inverse of such a power — and `hϖ₂` identifies its image in $C$ with $q$. The holomorphic ring `Omega.HolRingOf ϖ₂ ρ₂`, consisting of the functions on the upper half plane over $(K_0,C)$ that are holomorphic on each affinoid of the exhaustion attached to $\varpi_2$, is assumed to be a domain (`hdom₂`). Finally $dIso_2$ is a homomorphism from the decomposition subgroup of $A_2$ into the isometric automorphisms of $C$ over $K_0$, which by `hdIso₂` realises the natural action $\tau\bullet x$.
--
--   *The Hecke tower over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$.* A field $F_N$ is given, an $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-algebra which is a curve over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ in the sense of `IsCurveOver` and essentially of finite type over it, together with tower data $\mathbb T$ of type `HeckeTower.TowerData q q' FN`: a family of fields $\mathbb T.F\,\ell$ indexed by the primes $\ell\neq q,q'$, each a curve over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ essentially of finite type, together with $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-algebra maps from $F_N$ along the arrows of the index category which are finite and integral. The hypothesis `hfg` requires, for each object $j$ of the index category (the base, or a prime $\ell\neq q,q'$), an element $x$ of $\mathbb T$`.objField`$j$ transcendental over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with $\mathbb T$`.objField`$j$ finite-dimensional over $\overline{\mathbb Q}(x)$. Semilinear actions are given: homomorphisms $\mathrm{gal}_N$ and $\mathrm{gal}_T(\ell)$ from the decomposition subgroup of $A_2$ into the group of pairs (ring automorphism of the field, ring automorphism of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$) compatible with the structure map, whose base components are, by `hgalN` and `hgalT`, the given automorphisms of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$; and two distinguished semilinear automorphisms $W(0),W(1)$ of $F_N$ and $WT(\ell)(0),WT(\ell)(1)$ of $\mathbb T.F\,\ell$.
--
--   *Level groups and Atkin–Lehner elements.* For each prime $\ell\neq q,q'$ there are a unit $s_2(\ell)$ of $\mathbb H$ and a finite-idelic unit $sf_2(\ell)$ subject to `hs₂`: the component of $sf_2(\ell)$ at every place $u$ not containing $q$ is $s_2(\ell)\otimes 1$; its component at every place containing $q$ is $1$; the product of the diagonal finite idele of the scalar $\ell$ with $sf_2(\ell)^{-1}$ lies in `levelHeckeUSet Λ₁ S₁ ℓ` when $\ell\mid N$ — that is, in `primeHeckeSet S₁ ℓ` with the extra requirements that adelic conjugation by it does not preserve $S_1$ and that $S_1$ is not contained in the corresponding conjugate of $\Lambda_1$ — and in `primeHeckeSet S₁ ℓ` otherwise; and $\mathrm{nrd}(s_2(\ell))=\ell$, the reduced norm being $x_{\mathrm{re}}^2-a_1x_I^2-b_1x_J^2+a_1b_1x_K^2$. The subgroups $\Gamma_2(j)\le\mathbb H^\times$ are prescribed by `hΓ₂0` and `hΓ₂ℓ`: $\Gamma_2(\mathrm{none})$ consists exactly of the units $x$ that lie in `CosetGraph.awayUnits R₁ v₂` — the intersection, over all places $w\neq v_2$, of the preimages under localisation at $w$ of the subgroup generated by the local box units of $R_1$ at $w$ — and satisfy that $\mathrm{padicValRat}_q(\mathrm{nrd}\,x)$ is even; and $\Gamma_2(\mathrm{some}\,\ell)=\Gamma_2(\mathrm{none})\cap s_2(\ell)\Gamma_2(\mathrm{none})s_2(\ell)^{-1}$. Units $w_2(j),\overline w_2(j)$ of $\mathbb H$ are given with: `hw₂`, stating that $w_2(\mathrm{none})$ lies in `awayUnits R₁ v₂` and has reduced norm $q$, and that $w_2(\mathrm{some}\,\ell)$ lies in `awayUnits (meetOrder R₁ (sf₂ ℓ)) v₂` and has reduced norm $q$; and `hwbar₂`, stating that $\overline w_2(\mathrm{none})$ has reduced norm $q'$, that its localisation at every place $u\neq v_2$ not containing $q'$ is a local box unit of $R_1$ at $u$, and that for every $u\neq v_2$ conjugation $x\mapsto \overline w_2(\mathrm{none})^{-1}x\,\overline w_2(\mathrm{none})$ on the localisation at $u$ preserves the local box of $R_1$ at $u$ and the local box of $\Lambda_1$ at $u$, in both cases as an equivalence; the same three conditions are imposed on $\overline w_2(\mathrm{some}\,\ell)$ with `meetOrder R₁ (sf₂ ℓ)` in place of $R_1$ and $\Lambda_1$ unchanged in the last clause.
--
--   *The intertwining hypothesis.* Finally $\chi_2$ is a homomorphism from the decomposition subgroup of $A_2$ to $\mathrm{Multiplicative}(\mathbb Z/2)$, and for each object $j$ of the tower $\iota M_2(j)$ is a ring homomorphism from $\mathbb T$`.objField`$j$ to $\mathrm{Frac}(\mathrm{HolRingOf}\,\varpi_2\,\rho_2)$. The hypothesis `hI` asserts [`CerednikDrinfeld.DescentIntertwining`](def/CerednikDrinfeld_DescentIntertwining_v2.html#L17) for the residue parameter $q$ and the two Atkin–Lehner indices $0,1\in\mathrm{Fin}\,2$, applied to $A_2,\rho_2,\varpi_2,\Gamma_2,w_2,\overline w_2,s_2,dIso_2$, $F_N,\mathbb T$, $\mathrm{gal}_N,\mathrm{gal}_T$, $W,WT$, $\chi_2,\iota M_2$. This is a conjunction of many clauses, summarised here: $\chi_2$ is trivial on the inertia subgroup of $A_2$ over $\mathbb Q$, non-trivial at any Frobenius at $q$, and $\chi_2(\tau)=1$ holds exactly when $\tau$ fixes every element $x$ of the residue field of $A_2$ with $x^{q^2}=x$; each $\iota M_2(j)$ is compatible with the scalars, sending the image of $z\in\overline{\mathbb Q}$ to the image of $z$ in $C$; and the remaining clauses couple the embeddings $\iota M_2$, the Galois actions on the tower, and the Atkin–Lehner semilinear automorphisms indexed by $0$ and $1$ with the analytic data.
--
--   *Conclusion.* For every object $j$ of the index category (the base, or a prime $\ell\neq q,q'$), consider the invariant field $\mathfrak M(\Gamma_2 j)=$ `Mumford.invariantFieldOf C (ℍ)ˣ (HolRingOf ϖ₂ ρ₂) (Γ₂ j)`, the subfield of $\mathrm{Frac}(\mathrm{HolRingOf}\,\varpi_2\,\rho_2)$ of elements fixed by every $\gamma\in\Gamma_2(j)$. Then three assertions hold. First, $\mathfrak M(\Gamma_2 j)$ is a curve over $C$ in the sense of `IsCurveOver`: every non-zero element admits a divisor of degree $0$ whose value at each place is the order of the element there, the residue field of every place of $\mathfrak M(\Gamma_2 j)$ over $C$ is finite over $C$, and the module of Kähler differentials of $\mathfrak M(\Gamma_2 j)$ over $C$ is free of rank one. Secondly, $\mathfrak M(\Gamma_2 j)$ is essentially of finite type over $C$. Thirdly, there exists $x\in\mathfrak M(\Gamma_2 j)$ transcendental over $C$ such that $\mathfrak M(\Gamma_2 j)$ is finite-dimensional over the intermediate field $C(x)$ obtained by adjoining $x$ to $C$.
--
--   On the Čerednik–Drinfeld side of the comparison, this supplies the statement that the Mumford quotient of Drinfeld's upper half plane by each level group $\Gamma_2(j)$ of the Hecke tower is a one-variable function field over the completion $C$ at the place above $q$: it is a curve over $C$, essentially of finite type, and finite over a rational subfield. It is used by [`CerednikDrinfeld.exists_shimuraCurveModel_goodReduction_and_equivariantUniformization_pair_of_six_mul_dvd_of_neZero`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.exists_shimuraCurveModel_goodReduction_and_equivariantUniformization_pair_of_six_mul_dvd_of_neZero), where the curve structure of these invariant fields is the input to the equivariant uniformisation of the Shimura-curve models.
